            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniqueness+of+identity+proofs"} uniqueness of identity proofs
          %h3#omniscience_principles Omniscience principles
          %p
            There are also generalisations of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principles+of+omniscience"} principles of omniscience
            involving sets with tight apartness:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Every set with tight apartness has
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidable+tight+apartness"} decidable tight apartness
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LPO"} LPO
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+LPO"} analytic LPO
            %li
              %p
                Every set with tight apartness has
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidable+equality"} decidable equality
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WLPO"} WLPO
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+WLPO"} analytic WLPO
            %li
              %p
                Every set with tight apartness has
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+relation"} stable
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tight+apartness"} tight apartness
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov%27s+principle"} Markov's principle
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+Markov%27s+principle"} analytic Markov's principle
          %p
            The first one is equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            being a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boolean+category"} boolean category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingleton"} subsingleton
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝟚
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \to \mathbb{2}
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boolean+domain"} boolean domain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝟚
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{2}
            has decidable tight apartness is equivalent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            being either a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton"} singleton
            or an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            is a boolean category.
          %p
            The latter two are all still weaker than
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            being a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boolean+category"} boolean category
            since in general, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+of+truth+values"} set of truth values
            is only an set with tight apartness if and only if
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            holds.
          %h2#in_algebra In algebra
          %p
            Since sets with tight apartness have finite limits, the usual constructions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+algebra"} universal algebra
            apply; it's straightforward to define strong refutative inequality
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
          %p
            The various subsets that appear in algebra (such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroups"} subgroups
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideals"} ideals
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosets"} cosets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
