                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\in A
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+ideal"} Prime ideals
            are even more interesting. A two-sided antiideal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            (so also satisfying that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in A
            whenever
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x y \in A
            %em antiprime
            (or simply
            %em prime
            if no confusion is expected) if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \in A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x y \in A
            whenever
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in A
            %em not
            be a prime ideal (as normally defined). But in fact, it is antiprime antiideals that are more important in constructive algebra. In particular, an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+domain"} integral domain
            in constructive algebra is an inequality ring in which the antiideal of nonzero elements is antiprime.
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/apartness+relation"} apartness relation
            on a set with tight apartness
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is the same as the (strongly)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspace"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            on the discrete locale
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/apartness+relations"} apartness relations
            extends naturally to anti-algebra: an antiideal is the same as a
            %em closed
            ideal in a discrete localic ring that respects the closed equivalence relation corresponding to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo #
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \#
            = succeed "-topology" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo #
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \#
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is antiprime exactly when its corresponding closed localic ideal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi C
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{C}A
            is “prime” in an appropriate internal sense in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Loc"} Loc
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi m
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi C
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi C
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo ×
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi C
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m^*(\mathsf{C}A) \subseteq (\mathsf{C}A \times R) \cup (R\times \mathsf{C}A)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo :
                  %mi R
                  %mo ×
                  %mi R
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m:R\times R\to R
            is the multiplication. The fact that the complement of an antiprime antiideal need not be prime in the usual sense corresponds to the fact that taking the spatial part of sublocales doesn’t commute with unions.
          %p
            For more about apartness algebra, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antisubalgebra"} antisubalgebra
